# Production environment for the operator console (apps/web).
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` env vars are inlined at BUILD TIME by Next.js. This
# file is auto-loaded by `next build` / `next start` (Next.js convention:
# `.env.production` for `next build` + `next start`, `.env.development`
# for `next dev`).
#
# Why this file exists: the operator console runs in the browser
# (localhost or remote) and the apiFetch wrapper calls
# `${NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL}/api/v1/...`. If the env var is unset,
# apiFetch falls back to a relative URL which routes through the
# server-side catch-all proxy at `apps/web/src/app/api/v1/[...path]/route.ts`.
# That proxy forwards to `${API_INTERNAL_URL}/api/v1/<path>` on the
# server side, so the browser never needs to reach the API directly —
# works regardless of how the operator reaches the web (LAN, Tailscale,
# public IP, Funnel).
#
# Setting NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL to empty here enables the same-origin
# proxy path. The trade-off (vs an absolute URL) is one extra hop through
# Next.js, which is negligible for an operator console.

# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL is intentionally empty: apiFetch uses
# relative URLs that route through the server-side proxy
# (`/api/v1/[...path]/route.ts`). See comment block above.
# Set this to an absolute URL ONLY if the operator's browser can
# directly reach the API on the same network — e.g., LAN deploys.
# Default (empty) is the safe choice for Tailscale / Funnel / mixed
# access scenarios.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=